      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND/OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "We describe the effectiveness of liraglutide therapy in a severely insulin-resistant patient with type 2 diabetes mellitus (DM-2) requiring U-500 insulin."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "SUBJECT AND METHODS",
          "content": "A 65-year-old morbidly obese man (body mass index, 67.3 kg/m(2); weight, 156.2 kg) presented with a 20-year history of DM-2; the glycemic control deteriorated to U-500 insulin requirement. He was inadequately controlled (hemoglobin A1c [HbA1c], 9.1%) on metformin plus U-500 insulin titrated to 575 units daily. Liraglutide was added and titrated to 1.8 mg once daily over 3 weeks."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Insulin requirements decreased markedly (>50%) to 250 units daily after 5 months of added liraglutide, with a concurrent improvement in HbA1c from 9.1% to 6.9% and weight loss of 22.6 kg."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "The addition of once-daily liraglutide to the regimen of patients with uncontrolled DM-2 requiring U-500 insulin should be considered as it may help to reduce insulin requirements, improve glycemic control, and assist with weight management."
        }
      ]},
